<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chesapeake Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chesapeake Bay is the largest estuary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, extending about 300 kilometres from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> to the Atlantic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Its shores were home to Algonquian peoples such as the Powhatan and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a>, and the English colonies of Virginia and Maryland grew along its rivers from 1607 on tobacco exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay.jpg" alt="Chesapeake Bay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Landsat/NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chesapeakelandsat.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the English writer William Strachey he killed the chief of the Kecoughtan around 1597 and installed his young son Pochins in his place, and before 1607 he destroyed the Chesepians near the mouth of the <b>Chesapeake Bay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With the sanction of President Madison, Key went to Baltimore in early September 1814 and, with John Stuart Skinner, an American agent for prisoners of war, sailed down the <b>Chesapeake Bay</b> to the British fleet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a></li><li><blockquote>When John Smith met them in 1608 on <b>Chesapeake Bay</b> he described them as tall and well armed.</blockquote><a href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a></li></ul></section>
